node_modules/
*.db
*.db-journal
# WAL leaves two sidecars, not one: the log and the shared-memory index. The
# second only started appearing when the server switched the journal to WAL.
*.db-wal
*.db-shm
# No database is ever committed — not the real board, not the sandbox, not the
# derived vector stores. They are one machine's rows and one machine's churn,
# and a repository is not where either belongs.
databases/

.idea

# macOS Finder metadata
.DS_Store

backups/

.env
# Subagent-driven-development scratch: ledgers, briefs, review packages.
.superpowers/

.claude/

# Working material lives on this machine's disk and is never committed: the
# agent guides, the development notes, the course report, the plans.
CLAUDE.md
AGENTS.md
# Checks the guides' own numbers against the code, so it only means anything
# where a guide exists — on this machine, never in a clone.
tests/docclaims.test.js

# docs/ is private by default and publishes by name. It used to be `docs/`
# flat, which excluded the folder outright — and a tracked file's immunity to
# this list was the only reason the design record stayed in. That worked for as
# long as nothing new was ever added: a new file under docs/ was silently
# unpublishable, and `git add` refusing it is the *good* case. The screenshot
# gallery is what found this, since an ignored image is a broken picture in the
# README of every clone.
#
# So the rule is now `docs/*` — one level, which git can descend past — plus
# the list of what the repository publishes. A negation only works if no parent
# directory is excluded, which is why this is a directory allowlist and not a
# pile of `!docs/**` lines.
docs/*
!docs/cli-backends.md
!docs/security.md
!docs/release-checklist.md
!docs/decisions/
!docs/img/
README-Development.md
ROADMAP-Development.md
.vscode/
.ruff_cache/
.pytest_cache/
openspec/
.agents/
